TI  - MIGRATING HAEMULID FISHES AS A SOURCE OF NUTRIENTS AND ORGANIC-MATTER ON CORAL REEFS
T2  - LIMNOLOGY AND OCEANOGRAPHY
LA  - English
AB  - Juvenile french and white grunts (Haemulon flavolineatum and Haemulon plumieri) rest over coral colonies during the day and feed only at night in surrounding seagrass beds. The amount of N, P, particulate organic C and calories which these fishes deposited over the coral colonies that were their resting sites was examined. Weight-specific rates of N excretion by grunts decreased with increasing fish size. Rates of P excretion were not related to fish size. Excretory products were rich in N  (molar N:P = 48), primarily NH4+; fecal material was richer in P (N:P = 8). Feces leached over half of their P content within the 1st day. Half of the daily excretion and defecation occurred during the first 4 h after grunts returned to the reef, in which time they doubled the amount of NH4+ available to corals under calm conditions. Seasonal patterns of nutrient and particulate organic C (caloric) input to coral colonies varied with grunt biomass on the colony. The maximum input to  colonies of Porites furcata from grunts occurred during Aug., which coincided with the time of highest coral growth rate. Grunts deposited an average of 164 and 251 mg/m per day of particulate organic C (feces) on the P. furcata and Acropora palmata colonies over which they rested,an energy supplement of the colonies of 0.8 and 1.2 kcal/m per day. Rates of nutrient and organic matter input from grunts are comparable to or greater than rates observed in other naturally or artificially enriched ecosystems.
