AB  - Zooplankton excretion was estimated by combining biomass data with experimental laboratory data on excretion rates at 3 stations, characterized by low nutrient levels in the inshore waters of the Great Barrier Reef. Hourly ammonia excretion (as N) by net zooplankton (> 205 .mu.m) ranged from 11.9-22.6 .mu.g m-3 or from 1.6-5.0 g m-2/yr, and hourly phosphate excretion (as P) from 1.4-2.8 .mu.g m-3 or from 0.16-0.63 g m-2/yr. Hourly excretion of ammonia by microzooplankton (< 205 .mu.m) was 2.6 .mu.g m-3 (as N) and of phosphate 0.55 .mu.g m-3 (as P), values that were 15 and 27% of the excretion by net zooplankton, respectively. Combined excretion rates by net zooplankton and microzooplankton could supply only 9.0-29.2% of the N and 6.6-25.6% of the P for an assumed yearly primary production of C of 100 g m-2 (= 17.6 g m-2 of N and 2.5 g m-2 of P). Calculations from an empirical equation relating temperature to O2 consumption by a bottom community indicated a high potential for benthic nutrient regeneration in reef inshore waters (27.1 g m-2/yr, as N). The bottom community appeared to be the most important source of nutrient regeneration within the area studied.
